Smithay on Radix (ADR 0712). The file a keyboard's layout is given to a
program in is one of the kernel's files of memory (/devices/shm),
opened again for reading alone before its name is taken away: Radix has
no memfd, no seals and no shm_open. A picture's memory needs no
DMA_BUF_SYNC, which Radix has not: it is a file of memory, whose pages
are the same for everyone who mapped them. The address of a SIGBUS,
which says which program cut the pool of its pixels short, is read
through the libc crate's si_addr(), as its siginfo_t gives it on Radix.

